# CLOSE(2)

## NAME

close - close a file

## SYNOPSIS

```c
int close(int fildes);
```

## DESCRIPTION

Given a file descriptor such as returned from an *open, creat, dup* or pipe(2) call, *close*
closes the associated file. A close of all files is automatic on *exit,* but since there is a
limit on the number of open files per process, *close* is necessary for programs which deal
with many files.

Files are closed upon termination of a process, and certain file descriptors may be closed by
exec(2) (see ioctl(2)).

## SEE ALSO

creat(2), open(2), pipe(2), exec(2), ioctl(2)

## DIAGNOSTICS

Zero is returned if a file is closed; -1 is returned for an unknown file descriptor.

## ASSEMBLER

**Note:** v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here - a decimal call number and a `sys name; arg ...`
line. Neither is how a call is made on this machine. The gate is one extracode - **$77
SYS_close** - and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the error number
in r14.

**The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.** The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is
the only place a syscall number appears on the user side. See intro(2) for the whole contract.
